PARTITION FORMATTING

nix-shell -p gptfdisk       #  make sure partitioning utility is installed

########  setup disk partitions (SSD)

export DISK="/dev/nvme0n1"

# Zap all old partitioning info on SSD
sudo sgdisk --zap-all "$DISK"

# Create EFI (512M) and root (remainder) partitions with GPT labels
sudo sgdisk -n 1:0:+512M -t 1:ef00 -c 1:"EFI_SSD" "$DISK"
sudo sgdisk -n 2:0:0     -t 2:8300 -c 2:"rootfs-ssd" "$DISK"

# Format partitions with filesystem labels
sudo mkfs.vfat -F 32 -n EFI_SSD "${DISK}p1"
sudo mkfs.ext4 -L nixos-ssd "${DISK}p2"

# Mount with label-based device paths

sudo mkdir -p /mnt
sudo mount /dev/disk/by-label/nixos-ssd /mnt
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/boot
sudo mount /dev/disk/by-label/EFI_SSD /mnt/boot


NVIDIA PRIME OFFLOAD VERIFICATION (after switching nouveau -> nvidia, commit 68ff03d)

Run these after `sudo nixos-rebuild switch` + reboot into the new generation.

1) Confirm nvidia driver loaded (was nouveau before):
    nix-shell -p pciutils --run "lspci -k -s 01:00.0"
    -> expect: Kernel driver in use: nvidia

2) Confirm normal apps still use Intel by default:
    nix-shell -p mesa-demos --run "glxinfo | grep 'OpenGL renderer'"
    -> expect: Mesa Intel(R) HD Graphics 530

3) Confirm nvidia-offload actually switches the renderer:
    nix-shell -p mesa-demos --run "nvidia-offload glxinfo | grep 'OpenGL renderer'"
    -> expect: NVIDIA Corporation / Quadro M4000M

4) Confirm the GPU was actually engaged (not just claimed):
    nvidia-smi
    -> run while (or immediately after) step 3 is executing; the process
       should appear in the Processes table. NVIDIA may power back down
       within seconds, so check quickly.

Steps 2 vs 3 side-by-side (same command, different renderer string) is the
real proof the offload switch works.

ROLLBACK if anything looks wrong (black screen, X fails to start, etc.):
    sudo nixos-rebuild switch --rollback
    sudo reboot
Hard fallback if that doesn't even get you a working shell: at the
systemd-boot menu, arrow-select the prior generation (was 39 before this
change) instead of letting the new default boot, then Enter.

CAVEAT found 2026-08-26: step 1 above (`lspci -k` showing "Kernel driver in
use: nvidia") is NOT sufficient proof the GPU is functional. nvidia.ko can
still bind the PCI device and satisfy that check while NVRM internally
refuses to initialize an unsupported GPU (logs "NVRM: No NVIDIA GPU found"
in dmesg) and silently no-ops. Always check dmesg for that line too.


EXTERNAL MONITOR FIX: legacy_580 + reverseSync (2026-08-26, pending test)

Symptom: after the nouveau->nvidia switch above, external HDMI/DP monitor
plugged into the laptop's built-in port shows as `disconnected` in
`xrandr --query`, even freshly plugged.

Root cause (two parts, confirmed via dmesg on 2026-08-26):
  1) `nvidiaPackages.stable` had moved to 595.71.05, which dropped Maxwell
     (this laptop's Quadro M4000M / GM204) support. dmesg showed
     "NVRM: No NVIDIA GPU found" -- nvidia.ko bound the PCI device but
     never actually initialized it. See CAVEAT above.
  2) The external DP/HDMI ports are wired directly to the NVIDIA GPU, not
     Intel -- confirmed via the `HDA NVidia HDMI/DP` audio codec bound to
     PCI 01:00.1 (the GPU's audio function) in dmesg. Plain PRIME offload
     mode never drives physical outputs (it only feeds rendered frames
     back to Intel), so even a working NVIDIA GPU wouldn't light up these
     ports without reverse PRIME sync.

Fix applied in configuration.nix (not yet rebuilt/tested as of writing):
  - hardware.nvidia.package -> nvidiaPackages.legacy_580 (nixpkgs' current
    LTSB branch, covers Maxwell-Volta)
  - hardware.nvidia.prime.reverseSync.enable = true

Known risk: reverseSync is marked experimental upstream. Reported bugs
include X crashing on external-monitor unplug/replug, and possible issues
surviving sleep/wake with an NVIDIA-driven external display attached. A
runtime crash from this is NOT a broken NixOS generation -- just reboot,
no need for `--rollback`. Rollback only applies if the new generation
fails to produce a working session at all (black screen every boot, X
won't start, login loop).

CONFIRMED WORKING 2026-08-25: rebuilt + rebooted, external monitor detected
(`xrandr --listproviders` shows `NVIDIA-G0`). Persisted through MATE's
Displays app (see memory `project_nvidia_external_monitor_fix` for detail).

SLEEP/WAKE REGRESSION found 2026-08-27 (separate from the above): after the
nouveau->nvidia switch + reverseSync went live, laptop stopped waking from
`systemctl suspend` -- disk light + fan active, screen stayed black. Root
cause: `hardware.nvidia.powerManagement.enable` was not set. This is the
flag NixOS/nvidia docs call out as needed for the proprietary driver to
save/restore GPU state across suspend, and is specifically recommended
whenever prime offload/reverseSync is in play. Also flipped
`services.journald.storage` from `volatile` to `persistent` at the same
time, since the first occurrence of this bug produced no forensic logs
(hard-reboot wiped the volatile journal).

Fix applied in configuration.nix:
  hardware.nvidia.powerManagement.enable = true;   # NOT finegrained --
                                                      # that's Turing+ only,
                                                      # this GPU is Maxwell.
  services.journald.storage = "persistent";

CONFIRMED WORKING 2026-08-27: 3 `systemctl suspend` / wake cycles, all
clean (`PM: suspend entry (deep)` / `PM: suspend exit` in dmesg, screen
came back every time) -- 2 without the external monitor attached, 1 with
it attached (the reverseSync path). No black screen, no `err..alert`
journal entries tied to the resume.

One unrelated cosmetic issue surfaced during testing: `mate-settings-daemon`
segfaulted once (SIGSEGV in `libmatemixer-pulse.so:on_connection_server_info`)
